// **F217 traded two messages for one.** `clear`'s arm answered a Mutable cell and a Map with
// different sentences; `#[when_unmatched]` is one line for a whole set, so both facts now ride on
// the same line and this fixture and `clear_requires_builder_rejected` pin the same text with a
// different 'found'. That is F190's criterion biting: an arm answering per rejected type cannot be
// fully replaced. The resolver still names the four builder types, which is what the arm's first
// clause did, so what was lost is the *targeting*, not the content.
//
// And no `clear`, for the same reason there is no empty `mutable_new<T>()`: emptying a cell would
// need exactly the zero value `T` may not have. A cell always holds something, by construction.
fn main() {
    val c = mutable_new(1)
    clear(c)
}
